A 1.4 kg drop after three days of clean eating is almost never 1.4 kg of fat. It is water, glycogen, and a quieter gut. A 0.9 kg jump after a rest day is usually the reverse. If you only watch the scale, you will misread both.

PushLab does not estimate body fat. Use the scale as one input, then read it against how you trained.

## Fat loss is slow on purpose

Fat is energy. A kilogram of body fat is thousands of calories. A 500 kcal daily deficit, if you hit it, is in the neighborhood of half a kilo of fat per week—before you account for adaptation, weekends, and measurement error. Anything faster on the scale is mostly not fat.

That is good news. It means a bad restaurant dinner did not undo a month of work.

## Water moves fast

Water tracks carbohydrate stores, sodium, hormones, creatine, alcohol, and heat. Eat a high-carb meal after a low-carb week and glycogen refills. Water comes with it. The scale rises. Your waist may even feel tighter. Fat did not appear overnight.

The opposite happens when you drop carbs, start a deficit, or sweat a lot. The scale falls before your shirts fit differently.

## A simple way to read the number

1. Weigh after waking, after using the bathroom, before food or coffee, in similar clothes.
1. Write the number down. Do not negotiate with it.
1. At the end of the week, average the days you have.
1. Compare this week’s average to last week’s average, not Tuesday to Wednesday.
1. If training volume on your main lifts is stable or rising and the weekly average is drifting down, fat loss is probably happening even if one day spiked.

## What the scale cannot see

- A hard lower-body session can add intramuscular water for 24–48 hours.
- Constipation or a big fiber day adds mass that is not you.
- The luteal phase can hold extra fluid.
- Creatine loading is famous for a quick bump that is water in muscle, not fat.

If the only metric is weight, those look like “failure.” They are not.

## Pair the scale with a log

Record the session: date, lifts or cardio, sets, reps, load. When the weekly weight average stalls but you added a set of rows or ran the same loop faster, you have progress the scale hid.

That is the job of a workout log during a cut. The bathroom scale answers “what did water and gut content do this morning.” The log answers “did I train.”

## What to do this month

Pick one weigh-in protocol and keep it. Pick two or three lifts or a cardio route you will repeat. Review both on the same day each week. Ignore any single reading that sits more than a kilo away from your weekly average unless it lasts.

Fat loss is the slow line. Water is the jitter around it.
